# lemmas about þe primitive identity type `=`, whose only proof is `reflect`.
# matching on `reflect` replaceþ one side of an equation wiþ þe oþer.

## equality is preserved by applying a function.
show let leibnitz: [
  @α: *, @β: *,
  @a: α, @b: α,
  f: [α, β], reflect: a = b, (a f) = (b f)
] ≔ reflect

## reverse an equality proof.
show let symmetry: [
  @α: *, @a: α, @b: α,
  reflect: a = b, b = a
] ≔ reflect

## join two equality proofs.
show let transitivity: [
  @α: *, @a: α, @b: α, @c: α,
  reflect: a = b, q: b = c, a = c
] ≔ q

## a property of one side holdeþ for þe oþer. þe property is found by
## abstracting each occurrence of `b` in þe expected type, or wiþout one,
## each occurrence of `a` in þe type of þe second input.
show let transport: [
  @α: *, @a: α, @b: α, @property: [α, *],
  reflect: a = b, a property, b property
] ≔ {x ^ x}

## a computed value wiþ þe proof of what it is, so þat a case function can
## match þe value and still know it: in
## `e inspect { yea, known ^ …, nay, known ^ … }` each case seeþ `known`
## as `e = yea` or `e = nay`. þe property is found as for `transport`.
show let inspect: [
  @α: *, @property: [α, *],
  a: α, f: [b: α, a = b, b property], a property
] ≔ a f reflect
